  <section class="section">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      I visited Porto, Portugal in July for about a week. I flew from Amsterdam Schiphol and landed at Francisco Sá Carneiro Airport (OPO). Porto sits on the Iberian Peninsula and often flies under the radar relative to nearby destinations in Spain, southern Portugal, France, or Italy. I initially visited simply to add Portugal to my travel map, but I found a city offering strong tourist value: striking views, excellent port wine, historic buildings, scenic river cruises, and prices lower than many Western European hotspots.
    </p>
    <p>
      Porto is Portugal’s second-largest city and the capital of the northern region, functioning as a long-standing industrial and commercial center. The city feels industrial and aging, but with a visible student population and a tourism sector that is clearly growing. Architecturally, it ranges from medieval to modern, spanning numerous styles. Much of Porto is built on high ground overlooking the northern bank of the Douro River estuary, and its historic center was designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1996.
    </p>
    <p>
      The overall vibe is welcoming and relaxed, with high English proficiency, affordable rideshares, and impressive sunsets over the Atlantic.
    </p>

