  <section class="section">
    <h2>Understand</h2>
    <p>
      Porto reflects an important maritime legacy. 
      Its roots trace back to the Age of Discovery, when the city embodied grit, commerce, and shipbuilding rather than courtly prestige. 
      That legacy still shows: Porto historically supplied fleets, merchants, and labor, earning a reputation as a working city shaped more by trade than nobility.
    </p>
    <p>
      The culture felt warm, friendly, and Mediterranean-influenced despite the Atlantic location. 
      Locals often describe the city as proud, resilient, and no-nonsense an identity reinforced by its history of resistance and its nickname, "Cidade Invicta" (the undefeated city). 
      There is a palpable student energy, and street-performing university groups add character and movement to public spaces.
    </p>
    <p>
      Languages encountered included Portuguese, Spanish, and English. 
      English-speaking visitors should be able to navigate comfortably.
    </p>
    <p>
      Weather in July was warm and sunny, roughly 21 to 26°C (70 to 80°F), with no rain and mostly clear skies. 
      Despite the Atlantic influence, conditions were ideal for vineyard tours, sunsets, and river cruises. 
      Summer feels like the optimal season to experience Porto at its most relaxed and visually striking.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Gettting In &amp; Getting Around</h2>
    <p>
      I flew into Francisco Sá Carneiro Airport (OPO). 
      It was smaller than expected but easy to navigate. 
      Arrival was smooth for an experienced traveler, with no sense of congestion despite the airport’s growing role as Portugal’s secondary international hub. 
      Located roughly 15 km from the city center, it handles a high volume of passengers relative to Porto’s size, driven by increasing tourist demand and the region’s economic importance. 
      I took an Uber from the airport; rideshares in Porto were significantly cheaper than in many Western European cities, making airport transfers straightforward and cost-effective. 
      The Metro line E connects the airport to the city center.
    </p>
    <p>
      I relied mainly on walking and Uber around tourist areas. 
      Navigation apps make walking straightforward, but the hills are not trivial. 
      Much of Porto is built into steep, uneven terrain overlooking the Douro River, making it physically demanding to explore on foot but visually rewarding. 
      Even if you are relatively fit, expect some effort. 
      Mobility-limited travelers should factor in the gradients and prioritize transit options.
    </p>
    <p>
      Driving would be difficult and frustrating in central Porto, especially in tourist-heavy neighborhoods with narrow streets and irregular layouts, so I avoided renting a car altogether. 
      Walking works well for short distances despite the elevation, and rideshare filled the gaps efficiently and affordably.
    </p>
    <p>
      Public transit options exist, including trams and a modern light-rail system operated by Porto Metro, but I used them sparingly. 
      The metro is efficient for longer point-to-point trips, though it does not serve every tourist area equally, and announcements are primarily in Portuguese.
    </p>  

    <p>
      Overall, a combination of walking and rideshare was the most convenient way to get around, balancing flexibility with the city’s challenging terrain.
    </p>
    <p>
      Depending on your trip itinerary and interests, the Porto Card may be worthwile.  
      <a href="https://visitporto.travel/en-GB/porto-card" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        Porto.CARD
      </a>
      is Porto’s official city pass, providing free access to selected museums and public transport as well as discounts on certain attractions, services, monuments, and tours for 1-4 days.
    </p>

