        <strong>Sé do Porto (Porto Cathedral)</strong>
      </a>
      - One of Porto’s oldest and most significant monuments, with construction beginning in the 12th century. 
      Located on a hilltop overlooking the old city and the Douro River, it features a striking Gothic cloister adorned with traditional blue-and-white azulejo tiles.
      Porto is filled with churches, many of which charge an entry fee, and after a while they can start to blend together. 
      If you only choose one, this should be it. 
      Sé do Porto offers not just ornate relics, but also tiled cloisters, multiple levels to explore, a pavilion, and an open square outside with excellent city views. 
      Many churches are worth visiting, but this one stood out the most.
    </p>
