    <div class="section">
      <h2>Stay &amp; City Texture</h2>
      <p>
        I stayed in an Airbnb in Old Town Porto. 
        The location offered excellent access to restaurants and major sites, but at a premium price. 
        Central Porto has strong demand and relatively limited residential housing, which makes well-located rentals more competitive and expensive than expected.
      </p>

      <p>
        Quieter or more affordable lodging west toward Foz do Douro is a viable alternative if paired with rideshares. 
        That tradeoff sacrifices some walkability to major attractions but can offer better value, more space, access to the beach, and a calmer atmosphere, especially for longer stays.
      </p>

      <p>
        Porto feels layered and lived-in, a collage of historic granite, narrow cobbled streets, steep hills, and contrasting old-meets-new architecture. 
        The city possesses centuries of architectural styles alongside crumbling stone and abandoned buildings. Porto seems to thrive on architectural contradictions. 
        Few examples capture this better than its famous McDonald’s, housed in a beautifully restored 1930s Art Deco building protected by UNESCO. 
        Often cited as the most beautiful McDonald’s in the world, you have a large modern McDonald’s in a space that features crystal chandeliers and intricate stained glass.
      </p>

      <p>
        You don’t need a guidebook to notice the juxtapositions. 
        Just walk, look around, and let the jarring mashups reveal themselves.
      </p>

